<table  width="100%" border=2 bordercolor="white" bordercolordark="white" bordercolorlight="white" cellpadding=3><tr>	<td><FONT face="Arial" size="2"><BR><B><U>The Olympiad</U></B><br> <small>-<b>Shane</b> : <FONT face="Arial" size="2" color="black"><font color="#ffffff">Thursday, October 19, 2000</small><br><br><FONT face="Arial" size="2" color="black">Pantheons Herald<br>				Thursday, October 19, 2000<br>				Swifter, Higher, Stronger<br>				Citius, Altius, Fortius.<br><br>The Olympiad<br><br>The design of the Games of the MM T4C Olympiad events began many months ago. I had considered myself to be somewhat knowledgeable of the purpose and skeleton structured design after working with the Los Angeles Olympic Committee as part of the Rose Bowl task force for soccer. So, confident in fact, planning the &#8220;Games of the MM T4C Olympiad&#8221; seemed like a cakewalk.<br><br>I soon discovered my conceptual understanding was based upon &#8220;Modern Olympic Game&#8221; glitz and glamour&#8211;when the Olympiad event research tossed me deep into studying ancient history, thus exposing and educating one Wigglesnort to the purist ideologies, heavy religion affiliations, Olympic event history and demanding game design of any true Olympic venue.<br><br>My hope was to keep the T4C format as close as humanly possible to &#8220;the real ancient venue deal&#8221; and yet have the games be &#8220;fun and challenge&#8221; for each person in attendance.<br><br>All the while, Jungle II was addressing the technical restraints within T4C, giving every Event Overseer absolute control for creating and executing their personal Olympic events.  Darkon and I began contacting every T4C server owner-extending an open invitation of welcome to all. Behind the Mt Olympus curtain-some AWH GM&#8217;s took on double or triple duties-creating Olympic Village, setting up time line calendars programming event scripts and the game formats.<br><br>Hey, this all looked so easy on paper!<br><br>All the while I heavily prayed for no ISP or computer network failures. Tried to create T4C Olympic games with dignity and substance and trust in each server to walk into the games being somewhat delighted discovering a few new event surprises-yet leaving &#8220;we could do this better&#8221; egos at the door.  AWH GM&#8217;s worked overtime and a half creating more than just another CTF venue, one where game knowledge, teamwork and skill were key. Record keeping, budget balancing, answering email, setting up Mt Olympus server,<br>networking, finding terrific medals, writing copy, working with TimeViewer creating opening and closing ceremonies, touching base with GM&#8217;s while planning events,  working with closely Vircom somehow became &#8220;my life&#8221;&#8211;and I have enjoyed every minute of it.<br><br>My secret fears was the techno game god gremlins were going to rise up<br>against us as AWH was delivering into deep space-the first Olympic Event venue ever played solely within our solar system galaxy-completely devoid of physical landmasses-yet, &#8220;live T4C Olympians&#8221; would be &#8220;present&#8221; in a &#8220;Vulcan Mind Meld&#8221; sort of way. I wanted each one to feel &#8220;at home&#8221; on Mt. Olympus-and understand AWH was very glad you were here.. Heady thinking-if you believe in external existentialism.<br><br>So, this little AWH band of Olympic Overseers, formally were once known as the GM&#8217;s of AWH-began a journey onto Mt. Olympus-the normal way, visiting the local library reading and researching-then jumping on the net to tap into a global resources offered at just under a one billion sites. While T4C servers busily selected T4C gamers to represent them, I was researching ancient history..and became shocked when I began to piecemeal the &#8220;known&#8221;<br>history of the Olympics into one pile of knowledge.  Some of Olympic<br>published facts-are worth a second look-which I have elected to share with you, today. So hold on to your hats-a few of them you may find to be utterly amazing.<br><br>Obscure Olympic Facts of Life-aka Things The IOC May Not Want You to Know..<br><br>01) The Olympic Games were also known as the Panhellenic Gathering-which celebrated death to be part of sporting venue.<br><br>Control and ritual seem to be the defining terms. In coming to grips with the eternally present fact of death (remember: antiquity was a time of high infant mortality, death by diseases we can now control, and almost incessant warfare), the ancient&#8217;s put on shows where death was under human control. Sometimes the outcome of these shows was purposeful submission to death (as in the gladiatorial games); at other times, it was victory.<br><br>02) Since ancient times, Olympics are considered to be the highlight of religious ceremonies for one sect of peoples who follow Zeus as a true god. This sect takes an active participating role within all opening and closing of all Olympic Events-still today.<br><br>03) While married women were forbidden to attend the Games-and where put to death if discovered in attendance. Unmarried maidens were permitted. Women athletes were forbidden.<br><br>04) Crimes committed at the Games were considered sacrilege. Such crimes included the acceptance of payment, corruption or a wartime invasion.<br><br>05) Only free men who spoke Greek could compete within the games.<br><br>06) Cities are selected as Olympic Game Hosts rather than countries to<br>Olympic games-as it was in ancient times.<br><br>07) Winners at the games received wreaths made from the branches of a sacred olive tree.  Interestingly, the branches had to be cut with a golden knife by a boy whose parents were still living- The &#8220;living parents&#8221; part of the clause seemed to a key issue of great importance-why is unclear. The wreath was supposed to grant its wearer protection from Zeus-who was known for killing just about anyone. Though the wreath had almost no material value, its significance can be attributed to the fame attained by its winner.  An Olympic champion brought glory not only to the athlete, but also to his<br>village or hometown.<br><br>08) The first recorded games took place in 776 BCE and were a purely local event that lasted one day.  Having a ritualistic purpose, they consisted of sacrifices to Zeus in the morning followed by simple running races in the afternoon. You know-your common-as per usual, normal Olympic day. Sacrifices in the morning.. having a bit of a run before lunch J<br><br>09) The first Olympic event winner, Coroebus, was a cooking chef by<br>trade-but historically is known-as a runner.<br><br>10) Closing ceremonies today follow after a stad event. (Running)<br><br>11) Zeus is represented in all Olympics-ancient and modern-as the flame of the Olympic Torch.<br><br>12) Tattoos bearing the Olympic rings for the Sydney Games were considered &#8220;trendy&#8221;, however in an ancient times-it would be a reason for an Olympiad to be banned from the games-as being a sign of slavery.<br><br>13) Olympic victors were allowed to construct statues in honor of themselves in the Altis plain in 540 BC.<br><br>14) Although over 200 "countries" attended the Sydney Olympics, there are only 192 true countries on Earth.<br><br><br>AWH GMTO Planning Facts-Un-Inflated aka Things the IOC Doesn&#8217;t Know About..<br><br>1) The working task force in man-hours for Games of the MM T4C Olympiad-combined over five months was 4180 hours approximately-shared by 6 people working four hours per day-no weekends off-in fact-one Overseer delayed her vacation to avoid missing any events-in spite of her RL job as a Chief Hospital Administrator for one of the largest hospitals in California.<br><br>2) Working budget allotted for the T4C Games by Jungle II under $500.00<br><br>3) Budget spent by Jungle II for games-over five months-$1800.00 (whoops) forgot about that &#8220;extra T4C server computer&#8221; needed to run the game-and the phone bill. ROFL<br><br>4) Emails sent to Overseers, shared in list servers, sent to Vircom, T4C Owners, resend to clarified incorrect email addresses, contacting Olympiads and so on sent by LW-personally-without assistance-in five months-3211 by Outlook email counter<br><br>5) Emails lost in LW &#8220;Outlook Shuffle&#8221;-five known<br><br>6) Monies funded for Olympic events by Attending T4C Olympic Servers- none.<br><br>7) Overseers days&#8217; off-since the start of the Games of MM T4C Olympiad-zero<br><br>8) Olympiad Overseers who are glad to you here-EVERYONE OF US!<br><br>As the flames of the Games of the T4C Olympiad flicker into history, All With Honor would like to take one moment and thank you for attending the T4C Olympiad.  We have enjoyed every minute of the games-we hope you have as well. Thank you for calling your T4C home-Mt. Olympus-for this time.<br><br>You have served your RL T4C Game Server well during these games. May you return home to them transformed!  Hail Olympian!<br><br>This weekend-Games of the MM T4C Olympiad-Upcoming Events:  TIME CHANGES!<br><br>Sat 21st<br>						1PM Guild Feud-3 Person Team Event<br>						Event Overseer-KissyFace<br>                       			T1 Bar and Grill<br>                                    Medal Round- Bronze<br> 			 			Local Link versus Jungle II<br><br>						3PMGild Feud-3 Person Team Event<br>                               	Event Overseer-KissyFace<br>                                    T1 Bar and Grill<br>						Medal Round-Gold Silver<br>						Wizard Realm versus T4C Gamer<br><br>                        		5PM Olympic Bump Trivia-Final<br>                                   	Event Overseer-Nils<br>                                  	World Map<br><br>Sun 22nd 					3PM Running Event #3-25K Marathon<br>                         		Event OverSeer-MoonShadow<br>                         		Event Uniform-Free selection<br>                         		Event Begins in T1 Temple<br><br>Immediately to follow Event Race: 	Closing Ceremonies<br>						Game Event Winners<br>						Bug Report Winners<br>						Bump Trivia Final Winners<br>						Crown of Pantheon<br>						Golden Dragon Server<br>						Games of the MM T4C Olympiad-torch dimmed<br><br> <font size="-1"></td></tr></table><br>
